Should the test executable link the MiniShield MODULE directly?

A. No; link the reusable core and shared LLVM
B. Yes; MODULE is an ordinary executable library
C. Only if you copy Plugin.cpp into the test

Answer: A. The MODULE exists for runtime loading by opt. The core provides the directly callable implementation.

2. Parse tiny IR in a C++ unit test
===================================

Task: Future exercise: create tests/unit/CoreTest.cpp. Start with the helper definitions and one accept test below, then append the next chapters’ TEST blocks.

### The template to start with

```cpp
TEST(Eligibility, AcceptsSimpleDiamond) {
    llvm::LLVMContext context;
    // TODO: parse valid IR, assert success, verify, check eligibility.
}
```

### Step 1 — Separate parsing failure from test failure

parseAssemblyString returns a unique_ptr<Module> or null. Print SMDiagnostic on failure. ASSERT_NE stops this test before any dereference; EXPECT_NE records a failure and continues, which would be unsafe before dereferencing null.

### Step 2 — Verify the fixture before testing your code

LLVM’s verifier returns true for broken IR. ASSERT_FALSE(verifyModule(...)) means “this input is valid.” Otherwise a test might appear to reject an unsupported feature when it actually supplied malformed IR.

### Step 3 — Call the eligibility API

Get the named Function from the module and inspect both the decision and reason when relevant. For acceptance, stream the reason into the failure message so an unexpected restriction is visible.

### The full helper section

```cpp
#include "minishield/CFGAnalyzer.h"
#include "minishield/FlatteningPass.h"
#include "minishield/FunctionEligibility.h"
#include "minishield/StateMachineBuilder.h"
#include "llvm/AsmParser/Parser.h"
#include "llvm/IR/CFG.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Dominators.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Instructions.h"
#include "llvm/IR/LLVMContext.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Module.h"
#include "llvm/IR/Verifier.h"
#include "llvm/Passes/PassBuilder.h"
#include "llvm/Support/SourceMgr.h"
#include "llvm/Support/raw_ostream.h"
#include <gtest/gtest.h>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

static std::unique_ptr<llvm::Module>
parse(llvm::LLVMContext &context, llvm::StringRef ir) {
    llvm::SMDiagnostic diagnostic;
    auto module = llvm::parseAssemblyString(ir, diagnostic, context);
    if (!module) diagnostic.print("minishield-unit", llvm::errs());
    return module;
}
static std::string dump(const llvm::Module &module) {
    std::string text;
    llvm::raw_string_ostream out(text);
    module.print(out, nullptr);
    return out.str();
}
static constexpr const char *diamond = R"ir(
define i32 @choose(i1 %c) {
entry:
  br i1 %c, label %left, label %right
left:
  br label %join
right:
  br label %join
join:
  ret i32 7
}
)ir";
```

### The full first test

```cpp
TEST(Eligibility, AcceptsSimpleDiamond) {
    llvm::LLVMContext context;
    auto module = parse(context, diamond);
    ASSERT_NE(module, nullptr);
    ASSERT_FALSE(llvm::verifyModule(*module, &llvm::errs()));
    auto *function = module->getFunction("choose");
    ASSERT_NE(function, nullptr);
    auto decision = minishield::FunctionEligibility::check(*function);
    EXPECT_TRUE(decision.eligible) << decision.reason;
}
```

### Verify

```bash
cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build -R '^Eligibility\.' --output-on-failure
```

<details>
<summary>C++ — RAII, unique_ptr, and reverse destruction order</summary>

RAII binds cleanup to object lifetime. module owns its Module through unique_ptr; getFunction returns a borrowed pointer, not a second owner. Local objects are destroyed in reverse construction order: declaring context first and module second makes the module die before its context. Returning a module tied to a context created inside parse would leave a dangling dependency.

```cpp
llvm::LLVMContext context;       // created first, destroyed last
auto module = parse(context, diamond); // owns Module
auto *function = module->getFunction("choose"); // borrows Function
// Do not delete function or wrap it in an owning unique_ptr.
```

Checkpoint: Why can moving a unique_ptr transfer module ownership without extending the context lifetime?

</details>

Checkpoint: Explain the difference between ASSERT_FALSE(verifyModule(...)) and EXPECT_TRUE(decision.eligible).

### Check your understanding

Why declare the LLVMContext before the Module owner?

A. The context must outlive objects that use it
B. The context owns the test executable
C. unique_ptr copies both objects

Answer: A. Reverse local destruction order destroys the module first. Its LLVMContext must remain alive during that lifetime.

3. Test the graph and the analysis manager
==========================================

Task: Future exercise: append the graph test to CoreTest.cpp. Follow the same parse → verify → run → assert pattern.

### The template to start with

```cpp
llvm::FunctionAnalysisManager fam;
llvm::PassBuilder pb;
// TODO: register standard function analyses and CFGAnalyzer.
// TODO: request CFGAnalyzer, then assert hand-counted properties.
```

### Step 1 — Register the dependencies

CFGAnalyzer asks FAM for dominator and loop analyses. PassBuilder::registerFunctionAnalyses installs standard function analyses; registerPass adds your custom one. This test requests function analyses directly. A module pipeline needs the corresponding outer managers and proxy registration; do not blindly transplant this minimal harness into a module pipeline.

### Step 2 — Predict the graph

The diamond has four blocks, four edges, no natural loop, and no back edge. Entry dominates all its reachable blocks. Predecessor enumeration order is not an invariant; compare sets/counts when order is irrelevant.

### Step 3 — Check relationships, not labels

Name-based lookup is acceptable for a hand-written input fixture, but a transformation test should usually discover its new dispatcher from edges and instruction kinds. LLVM can suffix names to avoid collisions.

### The full graph test

```cpp
TEST(CFG, DiamondEdgesAndDominance) {
    llvm::LLVMContext context;
    auto module = parse(context, diamond);
    ASSERT_NE(module, nullptr);
    ASSERT_FALSE(llvm::verifyModule(*module, &llvm::errs()));
    auto &function = *module->getFunction("choose");
    llvm::FunctionAnalysisManager fam;
    llvm::PassBuilder pb;
    pb.registerFunctionAnalyses(fam);
    fam.registerPass([] { return minishield::CFGAnalyzer(); });
    const auto &cfg = fam.getResult<minishield::CFGAnalyzer>(function);
    EXPECT_EQ(cfg.blocks.size(), 4u);
    EXPECT_EQ(cfg.loopCount, 0u);
    EXPECT_TRUE(cfg.backEdges.empty());
    unsigned edges = 0;
    for (const auto &block : cfg.blocks) edges += block.successors.size();
    EXPECT_EQ(edges, 4u);
    const auto &dt = fam.getResult<llvm::DominatorTreeAnalysis>(function);
    for (const auto &block : function)
        EXPECT_TRUE(dt.dominates(&function.getEntryBlock(), &block));
}
```

### Extend it

- Add a natural loop fixture and identify the header/latch pointers. Check that the header dominates the latch and that the recorded edge is latch → header.
- Add an unreachable cycle to ensure the reachable-back-edge rule does not count it as an entry-reachable natural loop.
- Keep loop count separate from back-edge count: several latches may belong to one loop.

### Verify

```bash
ctest --test-dir build -R '^CFG\.' --output-on-failure
```

<details>
<summary>LLVM IR — Analysis caching and borrowed result lifetimes</summary>

getResult returns a reference to a cached result owned by the manager. const auto& avoids copying that result but does not freeze the IR. After a transform, invalidation may destroy or replace the cache. Do not retain the old CFG reference across mutation and then assume it describes the new graph. Returning PreservedAnalyses::all() after rewriting would falsely claim such caches remain valid.

See [Using the New Pass Manager](https://llvm.org/docs/NewPassManager.html) for manager registration and invalidation.

</details>

Checkpoint: Why can an arbitrary cyclic edge fail the natural-loop back-edge definition?

### Check your understanding

What must happen before CFGAnalyzer can request DominatorTreeAnalysis?

A. Register the required analyses with FAM
B. Create a second LLVMContext for every block
C. Count loop labels by spelling

Answer: A. The manager can compute only analyses it knows how to construct.

4. Assert that the dispatcher really controls the function
==========================================================

Task: Future exercise: append a builder test only after the complete Day 2 StateMachineBuilder exists. The fixture below is already prepared because it contains no PHIs or escaping instruction results.

### The template to start with

```cpp
// Snapshot original block pointers and the count after preparation.
// TODO: call builder, verify, discover dispatcher via entry successor.
// TODO: compare switch destinations against original non-entry blocks.
```

### Step 1 — Snapshot at the right checkpoint

Value demotion may split edges. Record the block count immediately before builder construction, after preparation. This design appends dispatch and invalid: the count increases by two, not one block per state. The switch contains one case per prepared non-entry block.

### Step 2 — Follow the live entry edge

Get entry’s unconditional successor, then inspect its terminator. This catches a completely disconnected fake dispatcher. Verify every expected state appears once and the default is terminal unreachable.

### Step 3 — Preserve terminal behavior

Count or identify the original return terminators. More complete tests should inspect every original branch transition’s state store and final edge, along with both conditional outcomes in behavioral tests. The unit test below is a starting structural assertion, not complete coverage.

### The full structural test

```cpp
#include <vector>
TEST(Builder, DispatchesOriginalStates) {
    llvm::LLVMContext context;
    auto module = parse(context, diamond);
    ASSERT_NE(module, nullptr);
    ASSERT_FALSE(llvm::verifyModule(*module, &llvm::errs()));
    auto &function = *module->getFunction("choose");
    auto decision = minishield::FunctionEligibility::check(function);
    ASSERT_TRUE(decision.eligible) << decision.reason;
    // This fixture has no PHIs or escaping instruction results: already prepared.
    auto *entry = &function.getEntryBlock();
    std::vector<llvm::BasicBlock *> original;
    for (auto &block : function) original.push_back(&block);
    const auto preparedCount = function.size();
    ASSERT_TRUE(minishield::StateMachineBuilder::build(function));
    ASSERT_FALSE(llvm::verifyModule(*module, &llvm::errs()));
    EXPECT_EQ(&function.getEntryBlock(), entry);
    EXPECT_EQ(function.size(), preparedCount + 2);
    auto *entryBranch = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::UncondBrInst>(entry->getTerminator());
    ASSERT_NE(entryBranch, nullptr);
    auto *dispatch = entryBranch->getSuccessor(0);
    auto *stateSwitch = llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::SwitchInst>(dispatch->getTerminator());
    ASSERT_NE(stateSwitch, nullptr);
    EXPECT_EQ(stateSwitch->getNumCases(), original.size() - 1);
    for (auto *block : original) {
        if (block == entry) continue;
        unsigned matches = 0;
        for (auto oneCase : stateSwitch->cases())
            matches += oneCase.getCaseSuccessor() == block;
        EXPECT_EQ(matches, 1u);
    }
    EXPECT_TRUE(llvm::isa<llvm::UnreachableInst>(
        stateSwitch->getDefaultDest()->getTerminator()));
    unsigned returns = 0;
    for (auto &block : function)
        returns += llvm::isa<llvm::ReturnInst>(block.getTerminator());
    EXPECT_EQ(returns, 1u);
}
```

### Step 4 — Exercise preparation and construction together

The direct builder test deliberately starts with prepared IR. It cannot catch a missing DemotePHIToStack call in FlatteningPass. Append this complete pass test using a loop-carried PHI and an escaping increment. It asserts valid transformed IR, no remaining PHIs, a live switch shape, and a mutation preservation result. Add behavioral runs at limit 0, 1, and several positive values; this structural test alone does not establish the returned count.

```cpp
TEST(Flattening, PreparesLoopCarriedPhi) {
    llvm::LLVMContext context;
    auto module = parse(context, R"ir(
        define i32 @count_to(i32 %limit) {
        entry:
          br label %header
        header:
          %i = phi i32 [ 0, %entry ], [ %next, %body ]
          %more = icmp slt i32 %i, %limit
          br i1 %more, label %body, label %exit
        body:
          %next = add i32 %i, 1
          br label %header
        exit:
          ret i32 %i
        }
    )ir");
    ASSERT_NE(module, nullptr);
    ASSERT_FALSE(llvm::verifyModule(*module, &llvm::errs()));
    auto &function = *module->getFunction("count_to");
    llvm::FunctionAnalysisManager fam;
    llvm::PassBuilder pb;
    pb.registerFunctionAnalyses(fam);
    auto preserved = minishield::FlatteningPass().run(function, fam);
    EXPECT_FALSE(preserved.areAllPreserved());
    ASSERT_FALSE(llvm::verifyModule(*module, &llvm::errs()));
    unsigned phis = 0, switches = 0;
    for (const auto &block : function) {
        switches += llvm::isa<llvm::SwitchInst>(block.getTerminator());
        for (const auto &instruction : block)
            phis += llvm::isa<llvm::PHINode>(instruction);
    }
    EXPECT_EQ(phis, 0u);
    EXPECT_EQ(switches, 1u);
    // Direct run() calls do not perform manager invalidation for you.
    fam.invalidate(function, preserved);
}
```

### Verify

```bash
ctest --test-dir build -R '^Builder\.' --output-on-failure
```

<details>
<summary>C++ — Pointers as object identity, not ownership</summary>

The original vector stores addresses of existing BasicBlock objects. Comparing those pointers asks whether a case targets that same object, regardless of renaming. The vector owns only pointer values; it neither copies nor destroys blocks. This works because this builder appends blocks and erases terminators, not the original blocks. Erasing a block would invalidate the corresponding stored pointer.

Checkpoint: Which snapshot survives renaming, and which snapshot does not survive deletion?

</details>

Checkpoint: How could a test that only searches for the text “switch” pass on a broken implementation?

### Check your understanding

When is the expected N + 2 block invariant measured?

A. N is the count after preparation, just before this builder
B. N is always the source-language statement count
C. N is the unprepared fixture count even if edges split

Answer: A. Preparation may add blocks. The builder invariant starts after that work.

5. Make rejection tests prove that nothing changed
==================================================

Task: Future exercise: add the dynamic-allocation rejection test. Then create isolated EH, coroutine, address-taken, intrinsic, and non-scalar-result cases.

### The template to start with

```cpp
const auto before = dump(*module);
// TODO: check eligibility decision and the specific reason.
// TODO: run the pass, verify unchanged module and preserved analyses.
```

### Step 1 — Isolate the unsupported feature

A variable-sized entry alloca is valid IR but outside the strict Day 2 contract. The fixture has a real body and an ordinary branch so an earlier declaration/terminator rejection does not mask the allocation rule.

### Step 2 — Compare parsed canonical output

Printing the same module before and after avoids formatting differences between handwritten source and LLVM’s printer. Assert exact equality for rejection; accepted transformations require different structural and behavioral checks.

### Step 3 — Distinguish direct calls from scheduled execution

Calling pass.run directly exercises its logic without pass-manager optional-skipping rules. An opt integration test must independently show that the plugin runs on the intended function. A test on an optnone function may otherwise pass an unchanged check simply because LLVM skipped the pass.

### The full negative test

```cpp
TEST(Flattening, RejectsDynamicAllocaWithoutMutation) {
    llvm::LLVMContext context;
    auto module = parse(context, R"ir(
        define i32 @dynamic(i32 %n) {
        entry:
          %storage = alloca i8, i32 %n
          br label %done
        done:
          ret i32 %n
        }
    )ir");
    ASSERT_NE(module, nullptr);
    ASSERT_FALSE(llvm::verifyModule(*module, &llvm::errs()));
    auto &function = *module->getFunction("dynamic");
    const auto before = dump(*module);
    auto decision = minishield::FunctionEligibility::check(function);
    EXPECT_FALSE(decision.eligible);
    EXPECT_EQ(decision.reason, "only static entry allocas are supported");
    llvm::FunctionAnalysisManager fam;
    minishield::FlatteningPass pass;
    auto preserved = pass.run(function, fam);
    EXPECT_TRUE(preserved.areAllPreserved());
    EXPECT_EQ(dump(*module), before);
    EXPECT_FALSE(llvm::verifyModule(*module, &llvm::errs()));
}
```

### Verify

```bash
ctest --test-dir build -R '^Flattening\.' --output-on-failure
```

<details>
<summary>LLVM IR — Preserved analyses are a promise, not a diagnostic</summary>

PreservedAnalyses::all() does not undo mutations. It tells the manager its caches remain valid. A rejected function can return all because it was not changed. A transformed function normally returns none in this prototype; a more selective preservation claim requires actually maintaining those analyses.

Checkpoint: Why would returning all after a broken partial edit make the failure harder to diagnose?

</details>

Checkpoint: How would a missing preflight check be caught even if the final module still verifies?

### Check your understanding

Why compare the exact rejection reason?

A. To ensure an earlier unrelated check is not masking the targeted rule
B. Because LLVM requires English reasons
C. Because any failure automatically proves the right rejection

Answer: A. A negative test should fail when the specific guard it targets is removed.

6. Run differential cases with a reproducible seed
==================================================

Task: Future exercise: write tests/integration/differential.py yourself from this reference. First write the discount kernel and driver from Day 2 lesson 24, and build both executables using that common-IR pipeline.

### The template to start with

```python
# TODO: parse two executable paths, seed, sample count.
# TODO: boundary cases + deterministic random cases within the domain.
# TODO: run both with a timeout and compare exit/stdout/stderr.
```

### Step 1 — Define what is observable

This pure function driver prints a result and returns zero. Compare stdout bytes, stderr bytes, and exit status. Require zero success before accepting equality: two crashes or two usage errors must fail. Programs with files, time, randomness, or external side effects require a richer controlled oracle.

### Step 2 — Fix the domain and seed

amount is −1000 through 10000 and level is 0 through 5. The bounded multiplication stays within int range. Boundary cases ensure small inputs and default switch paths are always tested. A dedicated Random(seed) produces repeatable cases within this Python environment; persist the actual failing case as a regression rather than relying on cross-version PRNG guarantees.

### Step 3 — Bound subprocess execution

Pass an argument list without shell=True. A timeout is a failing observation, not proof of an infinite loop. Report seed, index, arguments, both observations, and tool versions. These known drivers produce small output; an untrusted or noisy workload would also need output limits.

### The full runner reference

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import argparse
import random
import subprocess
from pathlib import Path

parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
parser.add_argument("original", type=Path)
parser.add_argument("protected", type=Path)
parser.add_argument("--seed", type=int, default=20261112)
parser.add_argument("--samples", type=int, default=100)
args = parser.parse_args()
if args.samples < 0:
    parser.error("--samples must be nonnegative")
programs = [str(args.original.resolve()), str(args.protected.resolve())]
rng = random.Random(args.seed)
cases = [(a, level) for a in (-1000, -1, 0, 1, 99, 100, 10000)
         for level in range(6)]
cases += [(rng.randint(-1000, 10000), rng.randint(0, 5))
          for _ in range(args.samples)]

def run(program, case):
    result = subprocess.run([program, *map(str, case)],
                            capture_output=True, timeout=3, check=False)
    return result.returncode, result.stdout, result.stderr

for index, case in enumerate(cases):
    try:
        left, right = (run(program, case) for program in programs)
    except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError) as error:
        raise SystemExit(f"FAIL seed={args.seed} index={index} case={case}: {error}")
    if left[0] != 0 or right[0] != 0 or left != right:
        raise SystemExit(f"FAIL seed={args.seed} index={index} case={case}\n"
                         f"original={left!r}\nprotected={right!r}")
print(f"PASS {len(cases)} cases; seed={args.seed}")
```

### Verify

```bash
python3 tests/integration/differential.py \
  build/differential/original build/differential/protected \
  --seed 20261112 --samples 100
```

Expect 42 boundary cases plus 100 generated cases only after those binaries exist. Then deliberately point one path at a program returning 1: the runner must fail. This verifies the checker, not just the pass.

<details>
<summary>LLVM IR — Undefined behavior can invalidate your oracle</summary>

Signed C overflow, reading uninitialized storage, and executing LLVM unreachable are outside the promised behavior. A mismatch on an invalid test input cannot by itself establish a transformation bug. Conversely, a pass must not introduce undefined behavior on an originally defined execution. Keep the domain explicit and validate fixture assumptions before interpreting failures.

