```

### Step 1 — Make arithmetic and work explicit

uint32_t arithmetic wraps modulo 2^32. All shift counts here are within 0..31, and rounds is fixed at 100. A changing state and printed checksum make computed results observable. This is a small synthetic workload, not a general predictor of application performance.

### The full kernel reference

```c
#include <stdint.h>
uint32_t benchmark_kernel(uint32_t x, uint32_t rounds) {
    uint32_t value = x;
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < rounds; ++i) {
        switch (value & 3u) {
        case 0: value = value * 1664525u + 1013904223u; break;
        case 1: value ^= value >> 7; break;
        case 2: value += i ^ 0x9e3779b9u; break;
        default: value = (value << 5) | (value >> 27); break;
        }
    }
    return value;
}
```

### Step 2 — Keep the driver outside the transformed module

Compile the C kernel alone to common IR. Link the same separately compiled C++ driver with original and protected kernels, without LTO. The driver’s chrono calls and library behavior are outside the prototype’s support scope. Separate compilation without LTO also prevents ordinary cross-module inlining into the timing loop.

### The full driver reference

```cpp
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
extern "C" std::uint32_t benchmark_kernel(std::uint32_t, std::uint32_t);
int main() {
    constexpr std::uint32_t calls = 20000, rounds = 100;
    std::uint32_t state = 0x12345678u;
    const auto begin = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    for (std::uint32_t i = 0; i < calls; ++i)
        state = benchmark_kernel(state ^ i, rounds);
    const auto end = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    const double seconds = std::chrono::duration<double>(end - begin).count();
    std::cout << state << " " << seconds << "\n";
    return seconds > 0.0 ? EXIT_SUCCESS : EXIT_FAILURE;
}
```

### The build reference

```bash
mkdir -p build/benchmark
clang -O0 -Xclang -disable-O0-optnone -S -emit-llvm \
  benchmarks/kernel.c -o build/benchmark/common.ll
opt -load-pass-plugin="$PWD/build/libMiniShield.so" \
  -passes='function(minishield-flatten),verify' \
  -S build/benchmark/common.ll -o build/benchmark/protected.ll
clang -O2 -c build/benchmark/common.ll -o build/benchmark/original.o
clang -O2 -c build/benchmark/protected.ll -o build/benchmark/protected.o
clang++-23 -O2 -c benchmarks/driver.cpp -o build/benchmark/driver.o
clang++-23 build/benchmark/driver.o build/benchmark/original.o \
  -o build/benchmark/original
clang++-23 build/benchmark/driver.o build/benchmark/protected.o \
  -o build/benchmark/protected
```

### Step 3 — Warm up, alternate, repeat, retain raw data

The Python harness measures the time reported from inside each process, not subprocess startup. It does one warmup for each executable, alternates trial order, checks every checksum, and reports all nine times plus median/min/max. Checksums detect many mistakes, not all; keep the differential suite. Increase calls if timings are too short relative to noise, using the same count in both binaries.

### The full runner reference

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path
programs = [str(Path(p).resolve()) for p in sys.argv[1:]]
if len(programs) != 2:
    raise SystemExit("usage: benchmark.py ORIGINAL PROTECTED")

def trial(program):
    result = subprocess.run([program], text=True, capture_output=True,
                            timeout=30, check=True)
    if result.stderr:
        raise SystemExit(f"unexpected stderr: {result.stderr}")
    checksum, seconds = result.stdout.split()
    seconds = float(seconds)
    if not (0 < seconds < float('inf')):
        raise SystemExit("invalid duration")
    return int(checksum), seconds

expected = trial(programs[0])[0]
for program in programs:
    if trial(program)[0] != expected:
        raise SystemExit("checksum mismatch during warmup")
values = [[], []]
for repeat in range(9):
    for which in ((0, 1) if repeat % 2 == 0 else (1, 0)):
        checksum, seconds = trial(programs[which])
        if checksum != expected:
            raise SystemExit(f"checksum mismatch repeat={repeat} program={which}")
        values[which].append(seconds)
medians = [statistics.median(v) for v in values]
for name, v, median in zip(('original', 'protected'), values, medians):
    print(f"{name}: raw={v}; median={median:.9f}s; "
          f"min={min(v):.9f}s; max={max(v):.9f}s")
print(f"runtime change={(medians[1] / medians[0] - 1) * 100:.2f}%")
```

### Verify

```bash
python3 benchmarks/benchmark.py \
  build/benchmark/original build/benchmark/protected
wc -c build/benchmark/original build/benchmark/protected
llvm-size build/benchmark/original build/benchmark/protected
```

Use the installed llvm-size or document a different size tool. Record its output format: the traditional text column can include read-only data in addition to executable sections. Inspect sections explicitly if reporting exact .text bytes. Do not publish timings until checksums agree and final code has been inspected.

<details>
<summary>C++ — extern C, duration types, and dead-code elimination</summary>

extern "C" makes the C++ declaration use C language linkage so it matches the C kernel symbol; it is not a request to compile the whole driver as C. steady_clock measures monotonic elapsed time. duration<double>(end - begin).count() converts the duration to seconds. A printed result makes the computation observable, but the optimizer may still simplify how that result is computed, so inspect the final kernel.

Checkpoint: Why would adding volatile to every variable change the experiment rather than simply make it “more accurate”?

</details>

Checkpoint: Which part of elapsed time does the Python subprocess wrapper exclude by reading the driver’s internal duration?

