14. Day 3 glossary
==================

### C++ and build concepts

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| RAII | Tie cleanup to object lifetime; local destruction runs in reverse construction order. |
| unique_ptr | An exclusive owner that can move but does not copy ownership. |
| Borrowed pointer/reference | Access to an object whose lifetime is controlled elsewhere. |
| Translation unit | A source file after preprocessing and inclusion, compiled as one unit. |
| ODR | One Definition Rule: constraints on declarations/definitions across translation units. |
| PIC | Position-independent machine code suitable for placement in a shared object. |
| Usage requirement | Build information propagated to consumers through a CMake target. |
| extern C | C language linkage for a declaration in C++; useful at C/C++ boundaries. |
| LTO | Link-time optimization; can optimize across separately compiled module boundaries. |

### Test and LLVM concepts

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Oracle | An independent rule for deciding whether a result is correct. |
| Unit test | An in-process check of a focused API or behavior. |
| Integration test | A check spanning components, such as opt loading the plugin. |
| Differential test | Compare original and transformed observations on the same inputs. |
| Property/invariant | A relation expected to hold across a family of cases. |
| Canonical IR | IR printed through LLVM’s parser/printer for stable comparison in a controlled setup. |
| Seed | Initial value controlling a reproducible generated input sequence. |
| Timeout | A bound on waiting, reported as failure rather than proof of nontermination. |
| Regression | A retained test for a known bug or behavior that must not return. |
| Minimization | Reduce a failing case while preserving validity, assumptions, and failure. |
| CTest / GoogleTest | CTest orchestrates commands; GoogleTest defines C++ test cases/assertions. |
| Analysis invalidation | Discard/recompute cached results that mutation made stale. |

### Measurement concepts

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Warmup | Untimed preliminary execution to reduce some first-use effects. |
| Median | Middle of sorted trial values; report alongside raw data/spread. |
| Runtime change | Here: 100 × (protected/original − 1), with matched units. |
| Checksum | A compact observable summary of work; useful but not a full correctness oracle. |
| Noise/variance | Timing variation from environment and workload effects. |
| Process startup | Launching/loading cost excluded by timing inside the workload driver. |
| Dead-code elimination | Removal of computations whose effects are not observable. |
| Final artifact | The optimized executable/object actually measured, not only pre-optimization IR. |
| Threat model | Explicit assumptions about an adversary; performance is not evidence of resistance. |

Checkpoint: Pick one term from each table and explain how using it precisely prevents a misleading completion claim.

### Check your understanding

Does a checksum match replace differential testing?

A. No; it is a useful workload guard with limited coverage
B. Yes; collisions are impossible
C. Only when results are fast

Answer: A. A checksum can miss differences and covers only its chosen workload.
